= Conclusion

As renewable sources of energy become more prevalent across the globe, grid energy storage capabilities have needed to expand massively to accommodate sources of energy that do not provide a constant output. While current battery technologies are effective for electronics, cars (gas and electric), and short term energy storage, batteries which can hold the energy for a long time while still being safe and reliable will become key. 

The zinc-ion batteries designed and tested in this experiment did not perform as well as even AA battery, but the changes and improvements seen in this experiment between iterations in emf, maximum current, internal resistance, and power output show that there is a lot of potential for zinc-ion batteries. In terms of safety, batteries with zinc sulfate as the electrolyte were safer, but zinc chloride batteries allowed for higher currents at the cost of stability and long-term efficiency. Iteration 5, which combined zinc chloride with manganese dioxide, had by far the greatest effective capacity and effective energy. In iteration 5, adding manganese dioxide to the cathode improved performance by stabilizing higher voltages for longer. 

Nevertheless, further research is necessary before zinc-ion batteries become a commercially viable product. If this experiment were to be redone with industrial-grade materials, longer discharges, many different electrolytes, and more cathode chemistries, substantial improvements could happen. With these improvements, zinc-ion batteries will help the world convert to renewable energy. These sources will rely on safer, cheaper, long-lasting zinc-ion batteries to create a sustainable world.
